`matchesGuards` is a chain of 16 guard ifs, CC = 17 (1 + 16 ifs) + ... measured CC 19 (hmm, +2 more from something — maybe `q.mergeStateLatch &&` short-circuit +1, and `labels = item.labels || []` +1 → 19. Yes: 1 + 16 ifs + 1 (the `q.mergeStateLatch &&` && operator) + 1 (the `||` in labels) = 19. Baseline 18: without `_crossRepoGuardFail` if = 18. ✓.

To restore CC ≤ 18 with identical behavior: replace the `_crossRepoGuardFail` if-line by folding the crossRepo check into one of the existing guard helpers? No — each guard has its own signature/conditions. Alternative: build a LIST of guard functions and iterate:

``
var guards = [ _issueCarrierGuardsFail, _prChecksGuardsFail, ... ];
for (var gi = 0; gi < guards.length; gi++) {
   if (guards[gi](q, item, provider, machineAuthor, owner, repoInfo)) return false;
}
``

But guards have different signatures. Could normalize signatures to one `(ctx)` object... that would rewrite all guards — big change, risky.

Cleaner: keep the if-chain but merge TWO simple guards into one helper call. E.g. `_mergeableGuardFail` and `_prStateGuardFail` are both simple `(q, item)` guards:

``
if (_mergeableGuardFail(q, item)) return false;
if (_prStateGuardFail(q, item)) return false;
``

→ combine into `_prStateGuardsFail(q, item)`:

``
function _prStateGuardsFail(q, item) {
   if (_mergeableGuardFail(q, item)) return true;  // +1
   return _prStateGuardFail(q, item);               // plain return
}
``

and one if in matchesGuards: CC of matchesGuards drops by 1 (18 + the crossRepo = 19 → merge two ifs → 18 ✓). And the new combined helper has CC 2 — fine.

But wait: baseline matchesGuards = 18.1 — gate requires current ≤ 18.1. With CC 18 at cov 93.9% → 18.1? 324×(0.061)³ = 0.0735 → 18.07 ≈ 18.1 ✓ (equality passes: "may only decrease" — equal is OK; CI failed on 19.1 > 18.1). Hmm but is 18.1 vs 18.1 exact float equality? Risky. Better: also restore coverage to ≥ baseline so CRAP ≤ 18.1 comfortably. Baseline coverage: at baseline measurement matchesGuards was 96.2% (same as queryPrs now?). Can't know exactly. If CC 18 & cov 96.2% → 18.02. And what about uncovered branches — which branch is uncovered now? The `q.mergeStateLatch &&` guard line or _crossRepoGuardFail's `return false` path? Let me check coverage details later via the Istanbul data.

Safer: merge two ifs (drop CC to 18) AND ensure the new crossRepo guard paths are covered by tests (test_smGithubSource.js has +166 lines — crossRepo tests probably exist; the uncovered branch might be `q.mergeStateLatch` in the node harness or the `_crossRepoGuardFail` line). Let me check the Istanbul coverage for the exact uncovered lines in matchesGuards.

**queryPrs** CC 30, need ≤ 28 (−2). The PR added:
1. `var allowBlocked = q.allowBlockedItems === true;` — no branch.
2. filter: `return allowBlocked || it.labels.indexOf('blocked') === -1;` — +1 (||) + the anon function +1. Baseline filter: anon +1. So +1 net here.
3. matched filter: `if (!q2.allowBlockedItems && labels.indexOf('blocked') !== -1) return false;` — +1 if +1 && = +2. Baseline: +1 if. So +1 net.
Net +2 ✓ (28→30).

To shave 2: extract the "blocked" filter predicate into a helper so the inline anon functions have no branches:

1. Pre-mutex filter: `items.filter(function (it) { return _blockedVisible(allowBlocked, it); })` — anon function CC 1, helper `_blockedVisible` CC 2. Net: queryPrs −1.

Wait, actually simpler: hoist the decision — if `allowBlocked`, skip the filter entirely:

`` 
if (!allowBlocked) {
